{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>His watchmen.</b> He says this concerning the false prophets who were in Israel, and they are like blind watchmen. And it is by way of parable, either about the day or about dogs that are mute at night, for they do not guard the house and there is no benefit in them." "<b>To bark.</b> The cry of a dog; and so in the language of Kedar [Arabic]." "<b>They lie down.</b> Like כלבים. And it has no cognate, for the heh is fundamental, as in הורים והוגים (Isaiah 59:13). For a verb does not come in the hif'il pattern except with a mem. R. Yonah said: the translator [Targum] rendered ניימין שכבין, because dogs cling to slumber more than all other living creatures. And the meaning is doubled [i.e., pleonastic]."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 56, :notes {2 "The lemma הזים is Ibn Ezra's reading; the received text has חזים (with chet). I preserve his form in translation. The word translated 'cognate' (ריע) literally means 'friend/companion,' here in the grammatical sense of a related root."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:59:53.852524035Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}